MAROC VERTICAL 2040: A Service-First Framework for Domestic Value-Added Capture in Complex Building Construction

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

This thesis advances a service-first framework for domestic value-added (DVA) capture in complex building construction, using Morocco’s 2030 infrastructure programme as the empirical environment. The central proposition is that the relevant unit of industrial policy is not the built asset but the annuity of services—installation, commissioning, maintenance, modernisation, spares, and training—that surrounds vertical-mobility systems over a twenty-to-thirty-year lifecycle. The thesis develops a falsifiable DVA measurement protocol that separates GDP-style value added from GNI-style resident-owned surplus, a procurement-forensics method for reconstructing how flagship contracts allocate the service annuity, and a pilot rule that treats DVA reporting as an inspection game rather than a declaration. It is grounded in verified procurement data from the Grand Stade Hassan II, the market structure of Moroccan vertical mobility, and the institutional constraints of payment discipline and audit capacity. The contribution is diagnostic, not predictive: it specifies how DVA should be defined, measured, and incentivised, and the conditions under which the proposed pilot would fail.

---

1. Problem Statement and Research Question

Morocco’s construction sector grew by 6.7% in 2025, and the 2030 World Cup pipeline creates a demand shock of unusual magnitude. The prevailing industrial-policy instrument is a 15% national-preference margin in public procurement under Decree No. 2-22-431. Article 147 applies this margin to the financial evaluation of bids from competitors not established in Morocco, operating through the reference-price mechanism. Article 148 reserves 30% of estimated contract value per budget year for very small, small, and medium enterprises established in Morocco. Article 146 provides a legal hook for industrial compensation—investment, skills transfer, training, local products, integration, and maintenance—in major projects.

The critical limitation is definitional: “established in Morocco” is an address-based test, not a DVA test. A foreign-owned firm with a Moroccan subsidiary qualifies. The preference can therefore tip a contract without producing domestic capability. The 15% margin operates only at the financial-comparison stage, and it may be disapplied where international commitments so require. It does not reach the subcontracting chain, where the value-added leak is concentrated, and it does not govern who holds the lifecycle service annuity.

The research question follows directly: Under what procurement, audit, and payment conditions can a verified lifecycle-DVA rule shift the equilibrium of complex-building procurement from foreign-specialist capture toward domestic service-annuity capture, specifically in vertical-mobility systems deployed through Morocco’s public-building pipeline? The question is conditional, not advocacy. The thesis specifies the conditions and identifies what would falsify them.

---

2. Theoretical Framework

2.1 The service-first unit of analysis

Complex building systems are not products with a single factory gate. A vertical-mobility installation is a bundle of design, components, software, installation, commissioning, and lifecycle maintenance. The DVA literature that treats value added at the point of manufacture therefore mis-specifies the object. The relevant DVA is the lifecycle value-added, defined over the asset’s service life, and decomposed into three layers.

Definition 1 (DVA decomposition). Let DVA be domestic value added over the lifecycle of a building system. Then

DVA = DVA_{\text{direct}} + DVA_{\text{indirect}} + DVA_{\text{lifecycle}}

where DVA_{\text{direct}} is Moroccan labour, domestically sourced components, and domestic engineering hours embodied in the initial installation; DVA_{\text{indirect}} is the value added by Moroccan upstream suppliers, computed via the national input-output table to avoid double counting; and DVA_{\text{lifecycle}} is domestic value added in maintenance, repair, modernisation, spares, training, and software over the asset life. Services value added is a decomposition of these three layers, not an additional addend. Counting a domestic maintenance provider’s labour as both a service and a direct component would double-count.

Two metrics are defined and reported separately. DVA-GDP includes all factor income generated within Moroccan territory, regardless of the ownership of the residual claimant. DVA-GNI includes only the operating surplus accruing to resident-owned capital; foreign-owned surplus is excluded. The choice of metric is stated for every calculation, because the policy implications diverge: a DVA-GDP preference can be satisfied by a foreign-owned assembler, while DVA-GNI cannot.

2.2 The procurement inspection game

A DVA preference is not self-enforcing. It invites shell suppliers, transfer-pricing games, and certification arbitrage. The procurement rule is therefore modelled as an inspection game between the contracting authority and the bidder. Let g be the gain from misreporting domestic content, p the audit probability, and F the penalty upon detection. Truthful reporting is a Nash equilibrium only if

p \cdot F > g

The condition is the binding feasibility constraint on any DVA preference. If audit capacity is low or penalties are weak, the preference will be captured by firms that are established in Morocco but do not embody Moroccan value added. The institutional record supports the concern: Morocco’s Inspection Générale des Finances has documented systematic use of fraudulent quality certificates, tax attestations, and technical references to secure public contracts, precisely in procurement categories that require numerous attestations and therefore expose gaps in documentary verification. Audit capacity is a hard gate on the pilot, not an administrative afterthought.

2.3 The service-annuity lock-in hypothesis

OEM maintenance is frequently protected by proprietary diagnostic software and restricted spare-parts access. This is a hypothesis from general industry practice, not a verified finding for Morocco. If it holds, “Otis trains Moroccan technicians” will not by itself create a replicable domestic service market, because the technicians will remain dependent on the OEM’s proprietary tools and parts. The hypothesis is tested in contract terms, not assumed. Where open-diagnostics and spare-parts access clauses are absent from the original equipment contract, the domestic service market is structurally foreclosed regardless of the number of trained technicians.

