3. Empirical Anchors

3.1 The Grand Stade Hassan II vertical-mobility package

The Otis contract for 125 vertical-mobility units—73 Gen2 elevators, 42 Link escalators, four moving walks, and six freight elevators—was announced on 11 November 2026. The contract value is reported at MAD 118.2 million in public-procurement tallies, though the award notice itself does not disclose a value; the public tender estimate was MAD 126.3 million. The award is treated as a completed procurement outcome, not a pending natural experiment.

The stadium’s contract structure provides the identification strategy. Domestic majors lead the large civil packages. The vertical-mobility package is the foreign-specialist contrast. The DVA question is not whether domestic firms can win prime lots; they already do. The question is where in the subcontracting chain the value added leaks, and who holds the service annuity after handover. The hypothesis is that the leak is at tier 2, not tier 1: the domestic prime contractor’s subcontracting network, and the post-installation service market, not the prime award itself.

3.2 Market structure: installation concentration, maintenance fragmentation

Between 2024 and 2026, 76 identified public contracts in vertical mobility represented MAD 265.7 million. Otis Maroc alone accounted for 41.3% of contract volume and approximately 88.3% of total value, with 22 of 34 installation awards. Its 2026 public-procurement value was driven overwhelmingly by the stadium package. Schindler Maroc and the Moroccan firm Relax Ascenseurs trail far behind in installation.

The maintenance segment presents a different structure. Moroccan firms—Aminas Ascenseurs, Horus Ascenseur, Atelier Moderne d’Automatisme, and others—hold a strong presence, often founded by former technicians of the major OEMs. Maintenance contracts are small and fragmented, typically in the tens or hundreds of thousands of dirhams per year. An industry expert estimates that 200 to 300 firms operate in the sector, with Turkish imports gaining share. The contestable DVA is therefore not the elevator cab or the traction machine. It is the twenty-to-thirty-year service annuity, and the institutional question is whether that annuity remains OEM-held through proprietary diagnostics or transitions to a competitive domestic service market.

3.3 Import dependence and the cost-gap question

Morocco imported $73.3 million of elevators and conveyors in the twelve months to December 2025, 10.1% more than the previous year, against exports of $2.0 million. Annual new-equipment demand is estimated at 4,500–6,000 units and is expected to exceed 7,000 in the medium term. New equipment represents roughly 55–65% of annual market value; modernisation, repair, and maintenance account for the balance. The domestic maintenance market is growing as buildings from the 1990s and 2000s enter modernisation cycles.

The cost-gap analysis must be conducted at the service boundary, not the equipment boundary. Independent maintenance providers internationally price 20–40% below OEM contracts, with parts mark-ups of 15–35% against OEM mark-ups of 40–80%, but they face constrained access to proprietary diagnostic tools and certain components. In Morocco, annual maintenance for a standard residential elevator ranges from MAD 6,000 to MAD 15,000, with comprehensive contracts including parts reaching MAD 18,000 to MAD 35,000. The relevant gap is not the landed cost of a new elevator against a Turkish or Chinese benchmark. It is the cost and capability gap between a Moroccan independent service provider and the OEM for the maintenance and modernisation of the installed base.

3.4 Payment discipline as a binding constraint

Payment delays in public construction remain a constraint on capital investment. The legal default under Law 69-21 is 60 days from invoice, with late-payment interest set at the central-bank rate plus 0.85% per month. Reported averages are lower in ministerial departments (approximately 23 days) and public establishments (under 38 days), but these averages conceal persistent outliers and do not capture the experience of small contractors on local-authority projects. In the second quarter of 2026, 28% of construction-sector managers reported difficult cash positions, and unpaid invoices on completed public works have left firms awaiting payment for extended periods.

A domestic firm considering a twenty-year service annuity will discount its expected cash flows for payment risk. The pilot must include enforceable payment timelines and penalties on the contracting authority, not merely on the contractor. Without payment discipline, the DVA preference is a nudge on a market that is already discounting the state’s credibility.

