4. Methodological Protocol

4.1 Deliverable 0: forensic reconstruction of the flagship case

The first deliverable is not a model but an accounting reconstruction. For the Grand Stade Hassan II vertical-mobility package, the protocol separates: (i) the tender estimate; (ii) the awarded contract value, where disclosed; (iii) the subcontracting chain at tier 1 and tier 2; (iv) the imported-content share by value and origin; and (v) the service-annuity holder for each system. The reconstruction uses public tender documents, award notices, and procurement tallies. Where contract-level data are unavailable, the protocol records the gap as a finding rather than imputing a value. The stadium is paired with a repeatable housing or public-building pipeline, where volume rather than height tests whether maintenance capability scales.

4.2 DVA measurement protocol

For each system i,

DVA_i = \sum_{j \in \text{Moroccan factors}} w_j + \sum_{k \in \text{Moroccan suppliers}} \nu_k + \sum_{t=1}^{T} \delta^t \, S_{i,t}

where w_j is factor income to Moroccan labour and resident-owned capital, \nu_k is the upstream value added of Moroccan supplier k computed via the input-output table, S_{i,t} is domestic service value added in year t, and \delta is the discount factor. The operating-surplus component is reported under both DVA-GDP and DVA-GNI. The “Made in Morocco” 40% local-value-added threshold is tested as a proxy, not adopted as the metric; for complex systems, a product-level threshold can be satisfied by assembly while the lifecycle service annuity remains foreign-held.

4.3 Cost-gap and capability-gap analysis

Before asserting that DVA capture is feasible in a target system, the thesis estimates the landed-cost gap between a plausible Moroccan maintenance/modernisation entrant and the OEM benchmark, decomposed into labour, parts access, diagnostic tools, certification, warranty risk, and financing cost. If the gap exceeds the 15% preference margin, the preference alone cannot tip the outcome, and the thesis must either select a different entry segment—maintenance and modernisation before new equipment—or argue for a different instrument.

4.4 Learning effects: downgraded to descriptive

A within-firm panel across a sufficient number of firms is infeasible within the doctoral timeframe, and cumulative output is endogenous to cost. The thesis therefore treats learning as a descriptive hypothesis and uses exogenous volume shocks—programme announcements and lot awards—as instruments where a credible first stage exists. If the instruments are weak, the learning hypothesis is reported as unidentifiable rather than asserted.

---

5. Policy Proposal: A Tripartite Pilot Rule

The pilot rule operates on competition structure, not origin, and it treats audit capacity and payment discipline as hard gates rather than soft complements. It consists of three elements, applied jointly to vertical-mobility packages in the repeatable pipeline.

First, mandatory unbundling. Flagship contracts are separated into lots for new equipment, installation, maintenance, and modernisation. The service lot is sized so that domestic maintenance firms can bid credibly. Unbundling does not require an origin test and is therefore less exposed to legal challenge than an expanded price preference.

Second, an audited DVA statement with an inspection-game design. Every bid above a threshold includes a DVA statement distinguishing DVA-GDP from DVA-GNI. The contracting authority audits a random sample with probability p, and the penalty F includes exclusion from public procurement under Article 152, which authorises temporary or definitive exclusion for false declarations and falsified documents. The pilot is feasible only if the design satisfies pF > g; if audit capacity cannot meet this condition, the pilot must not proceed.

Third, an open-diagnostics and spare-parts access clause. Original equipment contracts include a condition requiring disclosure of diagnostic protocols and non-discriminatory spare-parts pricing to accredited independent service providers. The clause is a condition of the DVA preference, not a separate regulation. It directly tests the service-annuity lock-in hypothesis: if the clause is accepted and enforced, the domestic maintenance market can scale beyond OEM-dependent technicians; if it is rejected or circumvented, the structural foreclosure persists.

The legal window for this pilot is narrowing. Article 147 already conditions the national preference on Morocco’s international commitments, and the United States–Morocco Free Trade Agreement imposes procurement thresholds on covered contracts—$6,725,000 for construction services—below which national preferences retain more room for manoeuvre. Morocco is not a party to the WTO Government Procurement Agreement, but its bilateral commitments define the boundary of any preference design. The pilot must therefore be screened against the procurement chapters of Morocco’s trade agreements before it is finalised.

---

6. Falsification Conditions and Limitations

The thesis is falsifiable on three pre-specified fronts.

Condition 1 (definition). If the DVA decomposition shows that the domestic lifecycle service annuity in the stadium package is already held by Moroccan firms at a level above the pilot threshold, the premise of foreign-specialist capture is false. The procurement tallies indicate the opposite for installation, but the maintenance position after handover is the relevant test.

Condition 2 (cost gap). If the landed-cost gap between a Moroccan maintenance entrant and the OEM exceeds the preference margin by a wide and persistent margin—specifically, if the gap is greater than 15% across two consecutive procurement cycles—the preference instrument is insufficient, and the thesis must recommend a different entry segment or a different instrument.

Condition 3 (audit). If the inspection-game condition pF > g cannot be satisfied because audit capacity is unavailable or penalties are not enforceable, the pilot is not feasible, and the thesis must say so. The IGF’s documented experience with fraudulent attestations suggests that the audit gap is real, not hypothetical.

The limitations are equally explicit. Contract-level data for the Otis package are not public; the DVA reconstruction will record gaps rather than impute values. The service-annuity lock-in hypothesis is tested in contract terms, not assumed. The learning hypothesis is descriptive, not causal. The payment-delay evidence is drawn from reported averages and survey responses, not from a contract-level payment series, which does not exist in the public domain.

---

7. Contribution

The contribution is a measurement and diagnostic framework, not a prediction. It specifies what DVA means for complex building systems, how it should be decomposed to separate GDP-style value added from resident-owned surplus, how the flagship procurement outcome should be reconstructed as a completed case rather than a pending test, and under what conditions a DVA preference is incentive-compatible. The framework is deliberately conservative: it treats the 15% preference as a necessary but insufficient condition, it treats the service annuity as the relevant prize, and it treats audit capacity and payment discipline as hard gates. If the pilot fails, the framework will have identified why it failed—definition, cost gap, audit, or payment—and that diagnostic contribution is itself the doctoral contribution.

