Appendix B. DVA Measurement Manual

B.1 System Boundary

The DVA metric is defined at the system boundary, not the product boundary. For a vertical-mobility system, the boundary includes design, components, installation, commissioning, maintenance, repair, modernisation, spares, training, and software over the asset life. It excludes land, financing, VAT, and pure resale. The system boundary is stated for every calculation.

B.2 Decomposition and Double-Counting Rules

DVA_i = DVA_{\text{direct},i} + DVA_{\text{indirect},i} + DVA_{\text{lifecycle},i}

where

DVA_{\text{direct},i} = \sum_{j \in \text{Moroccan factors}} w_j

is factor income to Moroccan labour and resident-owned capital embodied in the initial installation;

DVA_{\text{indirect},i} = \sum_{k \in \text{Moroccan suppliers}} \nu_k

is the upstream value added of Moroccan supplier k, computed via the national input-output table to avoid double counting;

DVA_{\text{lifecycle},i} = \sum_{t=1}^{T} \delta^t \, S_{i,t}

is domestic service value added in year t, discounted at rate \delta.

Services value added S_{i,t} is a decomposition of the three layers, not an additional addend. A domestic maintenance provider’s labour is counted once, either as direct factor income at installation or as lifecycle service value added in the year it is performed. Counting it in both places would double-count.

B.3 Two Metrics: DVA-GDP and DVA-GNI

DVA-GDP includes all factor income generated within Moroccan territory, regardless of the ownership of the residual claimant. DVA-GNI includes only the operating surplus accruing to resident-owned capital; foreign-owned surplus is excluded. The choice of metric is stated for every calculation. A DVA-GDP preference can be satisfied by a foreign-owned assembler; a DVA-GNI preference cannot.

Formally, let OS_i be operating surplus in system i, and let \rho_i \in [0,1] be the resident-owned share of that surplus. Then

DVA\text{-}GNI_i = DVA\text{-}GDP_i - (1-\rho_i)\,OS_i

B.4 Treatment of the “Made in Morocco” 40% Threshold

The 40% local-value-added threshold is tested as a proxy, not adopted as the metric. For complex systems, a product-level 40% threshold can be satisfied by assembly while the lifecycle service annuity remains foreign-held. The thesis reports both the proxy and the lifecycle DVA measure, and it treats divergence between them as a finding.

B.5 Discount Rate and Currency

All conversions state the rate and date. The reference rate is 1 USD = 9.97 MAD on 18 November 2026; the January 2026 rate was approximately 9.22 MAD/USD. The discount rate for lifecycle DVA is reported at a base case and in sensitivity; the base case is stated in the measurement manual, and the sensitivity range is reported alongside every lifecycle DVA estimate.

