Appendix C. Inspection Game Calibration and Audit Design

C.1 The Truthful-Reporting Condition

Let g be the gain from misreporting domestic content, p the audit probability, and F the penalty upon detection. Truthful reporting is a Nash equilibrium only if

p \cdot F > g

The condition is the binding feasibility constraint on the DVA preference. If audit capacity is low or penalties are weak, the preference will be captured by firms that are established in Morocco but do not embody Moroccan value added.

C.2 Audit Capacity Evidence

Morocco’s Inspection Générale des Finances has documented systematic use of fraudulent quality certificates, tax attestations, and technical references to secure public contracts, concentrated in procurement categories that require numerous attestations and therefore expose gaps in documentary verification. This is direct evidence that the audit gap is real, not hypothetical. The pilot’s audit design must therefore specify: the sampling frame, the audit probability p, the evidentiary standard, the penalty schedule F, and the institutional locus of audit authority. Article 152 of Decree No. 2-22-431 authorises temporary or definitive exclusion from public procurement for false declarations and falsified documents; this is the available penalty hook.

C.3 Calibration Protocol

The gain from misreporting g is estimated from the price gap between a compliant and a non-compliant bid in the relevant lot. The audit probability p is set by the contracting authority’s sampling rule. The penalty F is set by the exclusion schedule and any financial penalty. The pilot is feasible only if the design satisfies pF > g at the estimated values; if it does not, the pilot must not proceed, and the thesis reports the infeasibility as a finding.

