Appendix D. Legal and Institutional Screening

D.1 Decree No. 2-22-431: Relevant Articles

Article 147. Preference for competitors established in Morocco, operating through the reference-price mechanism of Article 44. A foreign offer is reduced by 15% in one case and increased by 15% in two cases; study services add 15% to the foreign offer. Exemption: a group with Moroccan-established member(s) at 30% or more. The test is established in Morocco, which is address-based, not a DVA test.

Article 145. For IT-systems contracts, non-established competitors must involve Moroccan experts at 20% or more.

Article 146. Industrial compensation may be included for major projects: investment, skills and technology transfer, training, local products, integration, maintenance and after-sales. No financial counterparty from the project owner. This is the legal hook for a DVA pilot.

Article 148. 30% of estimated contract value per budget year reserved for very small, small, and medium enterprises, cooperatives, and self-employed persons established in Morocco.

Article 149. Local-labour clause of up to 20% of the workforce.

Article 151. Subcontracting capped at 50% including tax, cannot cover the main body; the holder must use providers established in Morocco. The subcontracting preference is also address-based.

Article 152. Temporary or definitive exclusion from public procurement for false declarations and falsified documents.

An amending decree is in preparation, with gaps on national preference still open. This is the window for the DVA pilot rule.

D.2 Trade-Agreement Screening

Morocco is not a party to the WTO Government Procurement Agreement, which gives it more room to operate national preferences in public purchasing than the GPA would imply. The binding constraints lie in the procurement chapters of Morocco’s free-trade agreements. The United States–Morocco Free Trade Agreement imposes procurement thresholds on covered contracts—$6,725,000 for construction services—below which national preferences retain more room for manoeuvre. The pilot must be screened against these chapters before it is finalised.

D.3 DS513: What It Does and Does Not Imply

Morocco — Anti-Dumping Measures on Certain Hot-Rolled Steel from Turkey (WT/DS513/R) was an anti-dumping dispute. Morocco claimed Turkish dumping retarded the establishment of a new domestic industry. The panel found Morocco inconsistent with several WTO obligations, including the injury investigation. Morocco appealed, then withdrew after the duties expired in September 2019. The case is a lost case, not merely a fragile one. The lesson is that protection of an unestablished industry via anti-dumping requires a rigorous injury and retardation case that Morocco could not sustain. It does not imply that procurement preferences are equally fragile; the legal exposure differs by instrument. Turkey’s challenge to Moroccan steel standards is a separate, ongoing TBT matter and must be kept distinct from DS513.

