Appendix E. Market Data Tables

E.1 Vertical Mobility: Public-Procurement Concentration, 2024–2026

Between 2024 and 2026, 76 identified public contracts represented MAD 265.7 million. Otis Maroc accounted for 41.3% of contract volume and approximately 88.3% of total value, with 22 of 34 installation awards. Schindler Maroc and the Moroccan firm Relax Ascenseurs trail far behind in installation. The maintenance segment is fragmented: Moroccan firms—Aminas Ascenseurs, Horus Ascenseur, Atelier Moderne d’Automatisme, and others—hold a strong presence, often founded by former technicians of the major OEMs. An industry expert estimates 200 to 300 firms operate in the sector, with Turkish imports gaining share.

E.2 Vertical Mobility: Import Dependence and Segment Shares

Morocco imported $73.3 million of elevators and conveyors in the twelve months to December 2025, 10.1% more than the previous year, against exports of $2.0 million. Annual new-equipment demand is estimated at 4,500–6,000 units and is expected to exceed 7,000 in the medium term. New equipment represents roughly 55–65% of annual market value; modernisation, repair, and maintenance account for the balance. Annual maintenance for a standard residential elevator ranges from MAD 6,000 to MAD 15,000; comprehensive contracts including parts reach MAD 18,000 to MAD 35,000. Independent maintenance providers internationally price 20–40% below OEM contracts, with parts mark-ups of 15–35% against OEM mark-ups of 40–80%, but they face constrained access to proprietary diagnostic tools and certain components.

E.3 Steel: Capacity, Structure, and Trade Measures

Sonasid operates electric-arc-furnace production at Jorf Lasfar and Nador, with steel capacity of 800 kt and rolled capacity of 1.2 Mt/yr, producing rebar and wire rod. Maghreb Steel is the sole flat-rolled producer at approximately 1 Mt/yr, producing HRC, CRC, HDG, and prepainted products; revenue for January–September 2025 was MAD 4.42 billion (+15%). Univers Acier has approximately 700 kt of rebar capacity and has faced difficulty from 2024. Riva Industries has approximately 800 kt of special-bar-quality capacity, commissioned in 2024. There is no blast furnace; rolling capacity exceeds steelmaking capacity, and semi-finished imports account for 45–50% of import volume. Trade measures: a +19% duty on HRC until 19 June 2029, declining by 1 percentage point per year; anti-dumping duties on CRC from Egypt; provisional 50% anti-dumping duties on galvanised wire from Egypt and the UAE from December 2025. Demand is 2.75–2.82 Mt in 2026 and is expected to exceed 3 Mt for the first time in 2027; construction accounts for 60–65% of finished-steel demand. The DVA gap is not steelmaking as such; it is structural sections, heavy-plate grades, certified fabrication QA, environmental product declarations, and dependence on imported semis.

E.4 Aluminium: Import Dependence and Certification Bottleneck

Morocco has no commercially significant primary smelting. Unwrought import dependence is 85–95%. Profile import dependence is estimated at 60–80% (indicative, not precise). The façade question is certification and systems integration to EN 13830 and EN 14351, not tonnage. Aluminium extrusion capacity by firm and the façade certification trail are unverified and are recorded as gaps.

