Appendix F. Macro, Pipeline, and Enabling Data

F.1 Growth and Investment

GDP grew by 4.9% in 2025 (HCP). Construction and public works grew by 6.7%. The minister reports construction above 6% of GDP and about 1 million jobs; ministerial figures are cited as ministerial, not as HCP. Public investment in construction and public works rose by 56% in 2024 to MAD 64 billion.

F.2 Housing Pipeline

The housing deficit is approximately 300,000 units. The direct housing-aid programme had received 263,840 applications by 4 November 2026; 237,456 were eligible, 127,707 were assisted, and above 109,000 eligible applicants were still waiting. Aid is MAD 100,000 for homes up to MAD 300,000 and MAD 70,000 for homes in the MAD 300,000–700,000 segment. The target is approximately 300,000 beneficiaries by end-2028. If domestic capability cannot lower cost or raise quality in the MAD 300,000–700,000 segment, it is prestige capability, not industrial capability.

F.3 Seismic Code

RPS 2000 was approved by Decree No. 2-02-177 of 22 February 2002; the current version is RPS 2000 v2011. Draft Decree No. 2.24.766 (September 2024) amends it, revising speed and acceleration values and zones for certain communes. The open question is whether the 2024 amendment closes the gap identified in recent probabilistic seismic hazard literature, where peak ground acceleration above 0.35g in some areas contrasts with 0.14–0.18g in the code.

F.4 Self-Generation and Energy

Law 82-21 was promulgated on 10 February 2023. Decree No. 2.25.100, dated 5 March 2026, published in Bulletin Officiel 7489, entered into force on 9 June 2026. Surplus sale is capped at 20% of annual generation. ANRE Decision 04/26 sets the surplus tariff for 1 March 2026 to 28 February 2027 at MAD 0.21/kWh peak and MAD 0.18/kWh off-peak. The low-voltage tariff has not yet been set. The building business case is self-consumption, peak shaving, and resilience, not low-voltage surplus revenue. The model assumes 100% self-consumption; medium-voltage surplus at the ANRE tariff; low-voltage surplus at zero until ANRE sets the tariff.

F.5 Water

The World Bank reports renewable water per capita falling from 2,560 m³ in 1960 to about 620 m³ in 2020, approaching 500 m³ by 2030. The government plan targets 60% of drinking water from desalination by 2030, with 1.7 billion m³/yr of capacity. Any green-building claim must be water-first.

F.6 BIM Adoption

A Springer systematic review published in October 2026 summarising a prior national assessment reports BIM use in some major projects, with broader implementation at about 24% and wide variation by stage and stakeholder. There is no national BIM mandate. An MDPI 2026 qualitative study finds implementation predominantly at Level 1, with the absence of a mandate noted by all participants. The 24% figure is cited as a prior assessment summarised in the review, not as an original finding.

F.7 Office Market

Casablanca Class A rents are about MAD 250/sqm/month, with vacancy at 14.4% and about four months rent-free on a three-year term (Q1 2026 broker composite). The tower is not used to prove office demand.

F.8 AI 2030 Targets

Morocco AI 2030 targets by 2030: MAD 100 billion in added GDP value, 50,000 direct and indirect jobs, and 200,000 trained or certified persons. The first AI minister was appointed on 16 October 2026. These are targets, not achievements.

